{"name":"Foothill Drive","hex":"#cab48e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foothill","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hill at the base of a mountain or mountain range."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foothill"},{"term":"drive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To operate a vehicle:\n To operate (a wheeled motorized vehicle).","To operate (a wheeled motorized vehicle)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Planned, usually long-lasting, effort to achieve something; ability coupled with ambition, determination, and motivation.","Violent or rapid motion; a rushing onward or away;  a forced or hurried dispatch of business."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Utah State Route 186","blocks":[{"text":"State Route 186 (SR-186) is a state highway entirely within Salt Lake City, capital of the U.S. state of Utah. It forms a quarter-beltway connecting US-89 and I-15 leading north from Salt Lake City to I-80 leading east; as such, it effectively forms the missing (non-freeway) quarter of the I-215 belt route around the city, though it does not directly connect to I-215 at the north end. Despite this beltway role, the route passes through downtown Salt Lake City because downtown is built right up to the northern mountains surrounding City Creek Canyon. The portion of the route connecting downtown to I-80 is a high-capacity street heavily used by commuters, especially those travelling to the University of Utah or between downtown and eastern neighborhoods, but the portion on Capitol Hill north of downtown is much narrower and has sharp turns around the Capitol grounds. The roadway runs 9.34 miles (15.03 km) along Victory Road, Columbus Street, 300 North, State Street, 400 South, 500 South, and Foothill Drive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Utah_186.svg/330px-Utah_186.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah_State_Route_186"},{"title":"Foothills","blocks":[{"text":"Foothills or piedmont are geographically defined as gradual increases in elevation at the base of a mountain range, higher hill range or an upland area. They are a transition zone between plains and low relief hills and the adjacent topographically higher mountains, hills, and uplands. Frequently foothills consist of alluvial fans, coalesced alluvial fans, and dissected plateaus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Foothillsco.JPG/330px-Foothillsco.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foothills"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg/500px-Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg/1280px-Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg/500px-Utah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"SR-186 eastbound","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUtah_State_Route_186_eastbound.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/186bythecapitol.jpg/500px-186bythecapitol.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/186bythecapitol.jpg/1280px-186bythecapitol.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/186bythecapitol.jpg/500px-186bythecapitol.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_